  "result": "The node didn't pass: the gate returns **UNANCHORED_VARIABLE** on all three runs I used (3 of 6), and the cause is in the node itself, not something I can fix in Lean.\n\n**The defect (spans s1 and s3):** the rule has to pick out a traffic signal `d` and say the pedestrian faces it. The node ties that signal to the pedestrian only by comparing the signal head's position (`dev.position`) with the pedestrian's position and heading. The gate won't accept that as tying a device to the subject, whatever shape I gave the comparison:\n- **Run 1:** the comparisons as the node states them.\n- **Run 2:** added an implied distance bound written with an absolute value.\n- **Run 3:** added the same bound with the device position alone on one side of each comparison.\n\nThe gate's message names `dev_governs` as the way to tie a device to the subject, and the node doesn't claim `dev.governs`. None of the names it does claim can do the job, since `rel_occupies` only links road users to regions.\n\n**What the plan should change:** claim `dev.governs` and require that the signal governs the lane (or roadway) the pedestrian is crossing. That lane is already tied to the pedestrian through s4. The facing comparison stays as an extra condition on that signal. Alternatively, if comparing positions is meant to count, the gate's check disagrees with prompt rule 3b (\"a comparison that reads both\"). I've flagged that in `task_result.md` too.\n\n**State of the files:**\n- **Rule:** written as the node maps it. The run-2 and run-3 probe clauses are removed, so it matches what run 1 judged. I did not run the gate again after removing them; the harness will re-run it.\n- **Witnesses:** all five planned scenes are built, plus an extra `branch_2` where the signal shows a green left arrow alongside the circular green, so it isn't green \"alone\" (expected: not applicable). The gate stopped at the anchoring check, so it has never judged these scenes.\n- **`branch_1` caveat:** I couldn't read `NRS_484B_283_1d4_o1`, so `branch_1`'s pedestrian signal (showing don't walk, governing the crosswalk and lanes) is my guess at what makes that rule apply.\n\n**Choice I made:** no claimed atom links a lane to its roadway, so I wrote \"a lane of a roadway\" as the pedestrian occupying both, which is how the node lists that element.\n\n`task_result.md` is in the working directory.",
